      (from the ACS 5‑year file published the year *before* the births), and
    • pct_no_morbidity = births with Maternal_Morbidity_YN = 1 (“None checked”)
                         divided by total births
      (from CDC natality data).

Key joins / transformations
  1. Convert ACS geo_id to 5‑digit FIPS with LPAD so it matches natality FIPS.
  2. Label each poverty record with the target birth year:
        ACS_2015 → birth_year 2016
        ACS_2016 → birth_year 2017
        ACS_2017 → birth_year 2018
  3. Aggregate natality data by (birth_year, fips); compute pct_no_morbidity.
  4. Join the two CTEs and compute CORR for each birth_year.

Why ambiguity error happened before
  The column name Year appears in both natality tables; we must qualify it
  (nm.Year, tot.Year) inside the morbidity CTE.

BigQuery functions/features used
  • SAFE_DIVIDE to avoid division by zero
  • EXTRACT(YEAR FROM <DATE>) to pull year from natality “Year” date column
  • CORR(col1,col2) for Pearson coefficient
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────*/

WITH poverty AS (
  -- ACS 2015 → births 2016
  SELECT '2016' AS birth_year,
         LPAD(geo_id,5,'0')                                         AS fips,
         SAFE_DIVIDE(poverty, pop_determined_poverty_status)        AS poverty_rate
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.county_2015_5yr`
  UNION ALL
  -- ACS 2016 → births 2017
  SELECT '2017',
         LPAD(geo_id,5,'0'),
         SAFE_DIVIDE(poverty, pop_determined_poverty_status)
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.census_bureau_acs.county_2016_5yr`
  UNION ALL
